<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="letter"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Hum Reprod Sci</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">JHRS</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Journal of Human Reproductive Sciences</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0974-1208</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1998-4766</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Medknow Publications Pvt Ltd</publisher-name><publisher-loc>India</publisher-loc></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">21772748</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">3136076</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">JHRS-4-59a</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.4103/0974-1208.82367</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Letters to Editor</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Bromocriptine or cabergoline induced pituitary apoplexy: Rare but life-threatening catastrophe</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Singh</surname><given-names>Pratibha</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Singh</surname><given-names>Manish</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">1</xref><xref ref-type="corresp" rid="cor1"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Cugati</surname><given-names>Goutham</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Singh</surname><given-names>Ajai Kumar</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff4">3</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1">Department of Obstetrics &#x00026; Gynaecology, JIPMER, Puducherry, India</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>1</label>Department of Neurosurgery, JIPMER, Puducherry, India</aff><aff id="aff3"><label>2</label>Department of Neurosurgery, VHS Hospital, Taramani, Chennai, India</aff><aff id="aff4"><label>3</label>Department of Neurology, Dr. Ram Manohar Lohia Institute of Medical Sciences, Lucknow, Uttar Pradesh, India</aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1"><bold>Address for correspondence:</bold> Dr. Manish Singh, Department of Neurosurgery, Jawaharlal Institute of Postgraduate Medical Education and Research, Dhanvantri Nagar, Puducherry - 605 006, India. E-mail: <email xlink:href="manish007singh@yahoo.com">manish007singh@yahoo.com</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><season>Jan-Apr</season><year>2011</year></pub-date><volume>4</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage seq="a">59</fpage><lpage>59</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; Journal of Human Reproductive Sciences</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2011</copyright-year><license license-type="open-access" xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0"><license-p>This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-Noncommercial-Share Alike 3.0 Unported, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.</license-p></license></permissions></article-meta></front><body><p>Sir,</p><p>Prolactin secreting pituitary tumor is one of the common causes of infertility in males and females.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref1">1</xref>] Prolactin level is also high in non-prolactin secreting pituitary adenoma (e.g., growth hormone secreting) due to stalk effect.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref2">2</xref>] Most of the prolactinomas can be managed medically with bromocriptine or cabergoline which are largely used as primary treatment for prolactinomas, as they help to normalize serum prolactin levels and induce reduction in the tumor size, promoting restoration of gonadal function, cessation of galactorrhea and improvement in visual defects in the majority of patients.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref1">1</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref2">2</xref>] Bromocriptine or cabergoline induced pituitary apoplexy is a life-threatening complication which is rare but well known.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref2">2</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref5">5</xref>] This condition is characterized by sudden onset of headache, visual loss or deterioration, meningismus, altered mental status, and rarely, even death.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref1">1</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref2">2</xref>] This pathology is caused by hemorrhagic necrosis of tumor or pituitary gland infarction, in which pituitary function is compromised, necessitating rapid administration of corticosteroids and endocrine evaluation.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref1">1</xref>] Rapid surgical decompression is required if there is sudden constriction of visual fields, and/or rapid deterioration of acuity, or neurological deterioration due to hydrocephalus.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref1">1</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref2">2</xref>] These reports point toward the possibility of bromocriptine or cabergoline induced pituitary apoplexy, which should be kept in mind when a patient is neurologically deteriorating after starting these drugs. Careful follow-up is required when treatment with bromocriptine or cabergoline is attempted for prolactinoma in reproductive medicine clinic for infertility management in male and female patients.</p></body><back><ref-list><title>REFERENCES</title><ref id="ref1"><label>1</label><element-citation publication-type="book"><source>Greenberg MS Handbook of neurosurgery: Pituitary adenomas</source><year>2006</year><edition>Sixth edition</edition><publisher-loc>New York</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Thieme Medical Publishers</publisher-name><fpage>438</fpage><lpage>56</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref2"><label>2</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Knoepfelmacher</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Gomes</surname><given-names>MC</given-names></name><name><surname>Melo</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name><name><surname>Mendonca</surname><given-names>BB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Pituitary Apoplexy During Therapy with Cabergoline in an Adolescent Male with Prolactin-Secreting Macroadenoma</article-title><source>Pituitary</source><year>2004</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>83</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15761656</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref3"><label>3</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shirataki</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Chihara</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Shibata</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Tamaki</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Matsumoto</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Fujita</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Pituitary apoplexy manifested during a bromocriptine test in a patient with growth hormone and prolactin producing pituitary adenoma</article-title><source>Neurosurgery</source><year>1988</year><volume>23</volume><fpage>395</fpage><lpage>8</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3226523</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref4"><label>4</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pinto</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Zerah</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Trivin</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Brauner</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Pituitary apoplexy in an adolescent with prolactin-secreting adenoma</article-title><source>Horm Res</source><year>1998</year><volume>50</volume><fpage>38</fpage><lpage>41</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9691212</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref5"><label>5</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yamaji</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Ishibashi</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Kosaka</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Fukushima</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Hori</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Manaka</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Pituitary apoplexy in acromegaly during bromocriptine therapy</article-title><source>Acta Endocrinol(Copenh.)</source><year>1981</year><volume>98</volume><fpage>171</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7293648</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>